```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/gibbs-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

`lmm_animal_gibbs()` is a quick single-chain Gibbs sampler, meant for
simulations, teaching and sanity checks rather than for inference. Its priors
are inverse-Wishart on `G` and the residual covariance, which differ from those
of `lmm_animal()`, so the two posteriors are close but not interchangeable. It
does not need CmdStan.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
```

## Simulation

Half-sib families give a block relationship matrix without any pedigree code.

```{r simulate}
set.seed(1)
n_fam <- 40; fam_size <- 8
n <- n_fam * fam_size
family <- rep(seq_len(n_fam), each = fam_size)
A <- outer(family, family, "==") * 0.5
diag(A) <- 1

G <- matrix(c(0.60, 0.30,
              0.30, 0.50), 2, byrow = TRUE)
E <- matrix(c(0.80, 0.20,
              0.20, 0.70), 2, byrow = TRUE)
X <- cbind(Intercept = 1, sex = rbinom(n, 1, 0.5))
B <- cbind(c(1.0, 0.5), c(-1.0, -0.2))
Y <- X %*% B +
  t(chol(A)) %*% matrix(rnorm(n * 2), n) %*% chol(G) +
  matrix(rnorm(n * 2), n) %*% chol(E)
colnames(Y) <- c("t1", "t2")
h2_true <- diag(G) / diag(G + E)
```

## Fit

`iter`, `warmup` and `thin` count Gibbs iterations, not Hamiltonian
trajectories. Successive draws are autocorrelated, so the effective sample size
is much smaller than the number of draws; this run keeps 5,000 of 20,000
iterations and takes a few seconds.

```{r fit}
gibbs <- lmm_animal_gibbs(Y, X, A, iter = 20000, warmup = 5000, thin = 3)
str(gibbs, max.level = 1)
```

The draws are plain arrays: `G` and `R` are k by k by draws, `beta` is traits by
fixed effects by draws, `h2` is traits by draws. `fit_draws()` gives the same
named arrays as for the Stan models, with the residual covariance named `E`.

```{r draws}
gibbs_post <- fit_draws(gibbs)
round(draws_mean(gibbs_post, "G"), 2); round(G, 2)
round(draws_mean(gibbs_post, "E"), 2); round(E, 2)
round(draws_mean(gibbs_post, "beta"), 2); t(B)
round(rbind(estimate = draws_mean(gibbs_post, "h2"), truth = h2_true), 2)
```

## Summaries as posterior draws

Flattening the arrays into a draws matrix makes `posterior` and `bayesplot`
apply.

```{r posterior}
gibbs_draws <- posterior::as_draws_matrix(cbind(
  G11 = gibbs$G[1, 1, ], G12 = gibbs$G[1, 2, ], G22 = gibbs$G[2, 2, ],
  R11 = gibbs$R[1, 1, ], R12 = gibbs$R[1, 2, ], R22 = gibbs$R[2, 2, ],
  `h2[t1]` = gibbs$h2[1, ], `h2[t2]` = gibbs$h2[2, ]))
posterior::summarise_draws(gibbs_draws)
```

```{r trace, fig.height = 4}
bayesplot::color_scheme_set("blue")
bayesplot::mcmc_trace(gibbs_draws, pars = c("G11", "G12", "G22", "R11", "R22"))
```

```{r intervals, fig.height = 4}
truth <- c(G11 = G[1, 1], G12 = G[1, 2], G22 = G[2, 2],
           R11 = E[1, 1], R12 = E[1, 2], R22 = E[2, 2],
           `h2[t1]` = h2_true[[1]], `h2[t2]` = h2_true[[2]])
bayesplot::mcmc_recover_intervals(gibbs_draws, true = truth,
                                  prob = 0.8, prob_outer = 0.99,
                                  point_est = "mean")
```
